---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PlannerCTA from '../../components/PlannerCTA.astro';

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Where should sauna vents be placed?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Vent placement depends on the heater, room, and ventilation method. Current Harvia guidance uses different layouts for electric and wood-burning heaters and for mechanical and gravity ventilation. Follow the exact heater and room manuals rather than a generic diagram.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How much ventilation does an electric sauna need?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Current Harvia guidance and several Finnleo heater manuals use approximately six air changes per hour as a design target. The required vent locations and opening sizes still depend on the specific equipment and ventilation system.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does a wood-burning sauna use the same vent layout as an electric sauna?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Not necessarily. A wood-burning stove also needs combustion air, and current manufacturer guidance can use different inlet and outlet positions for gravity and mechanical ventilation. Use the stove and room instructions for the exact installation.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does every sauna need an exhaust fan?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'No single answer applies to every sauna. Some manufacturer layouts use mechanical exhaust, while others specify gravity or self-draught ventilation. The building pressure, duct route, heater, controls, and manufacturer instructions determine the correct system.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---
<Base
  title="Sauna Ventilation Guide: Electric, Wood-Fired, and Mechanical Layouts"
  description="Plan sauna ventilation by heater type and airflow method. Compare current Harvia, Finnleo, and TyloHelo guidance before following any vent diagram."
  canonical="/guides/sauna-ventilation"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-sm text-amber-700 font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide mb-2">Construction planning</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-stone-900 leading-tight mb-3">Sauna Ventilation: Match the Layout to the Heater</h1>
      <p class="text-stone-500">Reviewed August 2026 &middot; Planning guide, not an equipment manual</p>
      <p class="text-lg text-stone-700 mt-4">A vent diagram is only useful when it matches the heater, room, and airflow system. Electric and wood-burning saunas can require different layouts. Mechanical and gravity ventilation can, too.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden mb-8">
      <img src="/images/lifestyle/sauna-interior-window.jpg" alt="Sauna interior with benches and wall openings that require project-specific ventilation planning" class="w-full object-cover max-h-80" loading="eager" width="1200" height="800" />
    </div>

    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-xl p-5 mb-8 text-sm text-stone-700">
      <strong>Use the equipment instructions:</strong> The heater and sauna-room manuals control vent location, opening size, sensor clearance, and whether exhaust is mechanical or gravity-driven. A generic article cannot replace those documents or the local mechanical and building requirements.
    </div>

    <section class="mb-10" aria-labelledby="ventilation-first-decision">
      <h2 id="ventilation-first-decision" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">Start with two decisions</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-4 text-stone-700 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li><strong>Which heater?</strong> Record the exact model, control, temperature-sensor location, and whether it is electric or wood-burning.</li>
        <li><strong>Which airflow method?</strong> Determine whether the approved design uses mechanical supply or exhaust, gravity ventilation, or a packaged-room layout.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-stone-700 mt-4">Do not place vents first and choose the heater later. Airflow can cool a temperature sensor, disturb heater operation, or conflict with the room manual.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10" aria-labelledby="current-layout-examples">
      <h2 id="current-layout-examples" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">What current manufacturer guidance shows</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-stone-200 rounded-lg">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[680px] text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-stone-900 text-white">
              <th class="text-left p-3">System</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3">Example inlet guidance</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3">Example outlet guidance</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3">Critical check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-stone-700">
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border-b border-stone-200">Electric, mechanical</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Harvia places supply air in the upper part of the room.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Exhaust is placed low and away from the heater.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Keep airflow from cooling the temperature sensor.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-stone-50">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border-b border-stone-200">Electric, gravity</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Harvia places supply below or next to the heater.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Exhaust is low and as far from the heater as practical.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Use the model manual for dimensions and sensor distances.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-white">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold border-b border-stone-200">Wood-burning, gravity</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Harvia places fresh air low near the stove.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Its gravity example places the outlet high and far from the stove.</td>
              <td class="p-3 border-b border-stone-200">Coordinate combustion air, chimney, and drying.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="bg-stone-50">
              <td class="p-3 font-semibold">Packaged room</td>
              <td class="p-3">Use the supplied room openings and required wall clearances.</td>
              <td class="p-3">Keep the provided exhaust path unobstructed.</td>
              <td class="p-3">Do not push the room against a surface that blocks a vent.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-stone-500 mt-3">These are examples from current manufacturer guidance, not substitute specifications for another model.</p>
    </section>

    <PlannerCTA
      source="sauna_ventilation_after_layouts"
      heading="Turn the ventilation question into a complete project brief"
      description="Capture the heater type, location, dimensions, electrical readiness, and installation needs before asking a dealer or contractor for a layout."
    />

    <section class="mt-10 mb-10" aria-labelledby="why-diagrams-conflict">
      <h2 id="why-diagrams-conflict" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">Why two correct diagrams can look different</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        {[
          ['Mechanical versus gravity airflow', 'A fan or building exhaust changes pressure and air direction. A self-draught layout depends on temperature and pressure differences instead.'],
          ['Electric versus wood-burning heat', 'A wood stove needs combustion air. An electric heater may use a separate wall sensor that the supply air must not cool.'],
          ['Different heater and room systems', 'Finnleo manuals can specify an inlet below the heater and an outlet below the upper bench, while current Harvia guidance separates several layouts by ventilation method.'],
          ['Different discharge locations', 'TyloHelo warns that a self-draught system can fail when inlet and outlet terminate in spaces with different air pressure. The approved duct path matters, not only the grille position.'],
        ].map(([title, body]) => (
          <div class="bg-white border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">{title}</h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-stone-700">{body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10" aria-labelledby="ventilation-planning-sequence">
      <h2 id="ventilation-planning-sequence" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">A safer planning sequence</h2>
      <ol class="space-y-4 text-stone-700 list-decimal pl-5">
        <li><strong>Collect the exact documents.</strong> Get the current heater, controls, sensor, sauna-room, and ventilation instructions.</li>
        <li><strong>Mark the full air path.</strong> Show where supply air comes from, where exhaust goes, and which doors, ducts, fans, or adjacent rooms affect pressure.</li>
        <li><strong>Protect the sensor location.</strong> Keep supply airflow outside the prohibited distance stated in the heater manual.</li>
        <li><strong>Coordinate the building system.</strong> Check the plan with the equipment supplier and the qualified mechanical, electrical, chimney, and building professionals involved.</li>
        <li><strong>Plan drying.</strong> Follow the room instructions for post-use vent positions, door opening, and moisture removal.</li>
        <li><strong>Commission before routine use.</strong> Confirm that vents are open, the heater operates normally, controls read correctly, and no required opening is blocked.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-5 mb-10" aria-labelledby="ventilation-stop-checks">
      <h2 id="ventilation-stop-checks" class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mb-3">Stop and verify if any of these are unknown</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-700">
        <li>[ ] Exact heater and control model</li>
        <li>[ ] Mechanical or gravity airflow method</li>
        <li>[ ] Temperature-sensor clearance from supply air</li>
        <li>[ ] Required vent sizes and discharge location</li>
        <li>[ ] Wood-stove combustion-air and chimney plan</li>
        <li>[ ] Post-session drying method</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10" aria-labelledby="ventilation-sources">
      <h2 id="ventilation-sources" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">Primary sources used</h2>
      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        <a href="https://support.harvia.com/hc/en-gb/articles/21953036825628-Ventilation-in-the-sauna" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 hover:border-amber-400">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Harvia ventilation guidance</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-700">Separate examples for electric and wood-burning heaters and for mechanical and gravity ventilation.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="https://www.finnleo.com/hubfs/Finnleo%20Downloads/72-0111%20Piccolo%20Pure%20Heater%2012-19-2019.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 hover:border-amber-400">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Finnleo Piccolo Pure manual</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-700">Model-specific air-change, inlet, outlet, and opening-size instructions.</p>
        </a>
        <a href="https://www.tylohelo.com/hubfs/How%20to/Guide%20-%20How%20to%20build%20a%20sauna.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="block border border-stone-200 rounded-lg p-5 hover:border-amber-400 md:col-span-2">
          <h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">TyloHelo build guide</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-700">Self-draught airflow guidance and pressure-path cautions.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10" aria-labelledby="ventilation-faq">
      <h2 id="ventilation-faq" class="text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mb-4">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-5">
        <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Where should sauna vents be placed?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">Use the locations in the exact equipment and room manuals. The answer changes with heater type and ventilation method.</p></div>
        <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">How many air changes should I plan for?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">Current Harvia guidance and several Finnleo electric-heater manuals use approximately six air changes per hour. Confirm the target and opening sizes for the actual system.</p></div>
        <div><h3 class="font-bold text-stone-900 mb-1">Can I add a fan later?</h3><p class="text-stone-700">A fan changes room pressure and airflow. Treat it as a system change and verify the revised design with the manufacturer and qualified professional before installation.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="bg-stone-50 border border-stone-200 rounded-xl p-6 mt-10">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-stone-900 mb-2">Continue the build plan</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm">
        <li><a href="/guides/how-to-build-a-sauna/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Safe sauna build sequence</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-insulation/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Heat and moisture assembly planning</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-heater-sizing-guide/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Heater sizing guide</a></li>
        <li><a href="/guides/sauna-electrical-requirements/" class="text-amber-700 hover:underline">Electrical planning guide</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
